SUMMARY:Her Labour
DESCRIPTION:Women make up more than half of the construction workforce in India\, and usually earn half as much. This roundtable public workshop asks how we can contribute to building awareness and solidarity with women construction workers\, across the barriers of gender and class that may separate us. What connects us to the women toiling in construction?  \nThis workshop begins with a roundtable discussion between an architect\, a historian\, and a social worker reflecting on their engagements with women construction workers. Following the roundtable\, workshop participants of all backgrounds are invited to bring their own reflections and traces regarding women in construction\, from stories to poems\, drawings to objects. These will serve as material for a collective response and creative counter-archive to invisibility and indifference.  \nThis workshop is part of the project ‘Omitted from History: How workers on India’s Building Sites Mediated Twentieth-century Modernity’\, funded by the Horizon Europe programme (Grant No 101108229).  \nThis event is a part of the “Expanding Agency and Archival Silences” exhibition. Find the full schedule here.  \nIn collaboration with:\n   \nVenue: Seminar Halls\n\n\nEngagements With Women Construction Workers\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfQyywNhKH8MKOzcgiM748w0v5KBuFFuDAEe2SCq9RlFC9wGA/viewform?usp=pp_url)\n*Speakers*: Sarah Melsens\, Sharath R Nayak\, Prameela V\n*Event Categories*: Architecture\, Gender\, Visual Arts\, Workshops
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/her-labour/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
